course: dictionary
lesson: 6331
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words can mean different things. The word mould can be a noun (a thing) or a verb (an action), and each meaning changes what the sentence is about. Look at the words around it to find out which meaning the author means.
explain: «Leave a slice of bread in a warm, damp place and in a few days fuzzy spots of mould appear.» The fuzzy spots and the bread tell you mould is a furry growth. Now look at «Wax crayons are made of melted coloured wax poured into moulds.» Here moulds are hollow shapes that the wax is poured into.
explain: When mould is a verb, someone is doing something, like pressing soft clay into a shape. Ask yourself: is this a thing, or is someone doing something? That question sorts most meanings out, and then you can test your meaning in the sentence.
fact: Mould is a fungus that grows from tiny spores floating in the air.
fact: Mould likes warm, damp places, such as old food or damp walls.
fact: Never eat mouldy food, and do not sniff mould, because it can make you ill.
fact: A mould can be a hollow container, such as a jelly mould, that gives a liquid its shape as it sets.
fact: To mould means to press or shape something soft, like clay; «break the mould» means to do something new.
exercise: «Leave a slice of bread in a warm, damp place and in a few days fuzzy spots of mould appear.» What is the mould here? | *A soft, furry growth that grows on old food | A way of shaping clay | A kind of medicine | A hollow container for jelly
exercise: «Wax crayons are made of melted coloured wax poured into moulds.» What is a mould in this sentence? | A furry growth on the wax | *A hollow shape that the wax is poured into | To press the wax into a ball | A sheet of wax paper
exercise: The artist will ___ the wet clay into a small horse. Which word fits best? | jelly | mouldy | fuzz | *mould
test: «The Terracotta Army was made from shared moulds with details added by hand.» In this sentence, moulds means: | *Hollow shapes used to make the same figure again | Rules that must be broken | Green fuzzy growths on the clay | Small pieces of wood
test: Nadia says, «Her parents helped mould her into a kind, careful person.» What does mould mean here? | To leave her in a damp place | To grow fuzzy spots on her | To press her into clay | *To influence how someone grows up and becomes
exam: Which sentence uses mould as a verb? | The bread had a green mould on it | The mould grew quickly in the damp corner | *Sculptors mould the wet clay with their thumbs | The jelly mould is on the top shelf
